## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design lightweight governance frameworks for multi-team organizations with regional considerations, emphasizing practical artifacts, rituals, and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- Engineering governance
- Artifacts and rituals
- Metrics and experiments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple guardrail filter: decide if a feature request should be evaluated against guardrails
function shouldGuardrailsApply(request) {
  const highImpactPatterns = [/auth|security|data/i];
  return highImpactPatterns.some((r) => r.test(request.description));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate onboarding into concrete artifacts, rituals, and measurable outcomes for a distributed setting.

## Key Concepts
- Onboarding playbooks that scale across time zones
- Clear artifacts and acceptance criteria
- Lightweight rituals that sustain momentum
- Quantifiable ramp metrics and experiments

## Code Example
```javascript
function rampScore({timeToFirstCommit, issuesClosed, prMergeTime}) {
  const t = timeToFirstCommit <= 7 ? 1 : 0;
  const i = issuesClosed >= 5 ? 1 : 0;
  const p = prMergeTime <= 48 ? 1 : 0;
  return t + i + p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate onboarding checklists and artifact generation?
- How would you adapt artifacts for contractors vs. full-time engineers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical risk management when relying on external data and requires a concrete rollout plan across regions with resilience patterns and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and canary deployments
- SLOs and data freshness metrics
- Resilience patterns: circuit breakers, fallback caches
- Rollout artifacts, runbooks, and incident drills

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight circuit-breaker with fallback
class Breaker {
  constructor(threshold=3) { this.failures=0; this.open=false; }
  async call(fn) {
    if (this.open) throw new Error('Circuit open');
    try { return await fn(); } catch (e) { this.failures++; if (this.failures >= threshold) this.open=true; throw e; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ETA accuracy during canary without impacting users?
- How would you design post-incident reviews and remedial actions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manage dependency risk, reliability, and governance during a real vendor outage and migration. Candidate must balance rapid stabilization with a long-term strategy that minimizes disruption and preserves velocity.

## Key Concepts
- Dependency risk management with a vendor service
- Reliability-focused migration planning
- Cross-team governance (telemetry, incidents, release engineering)
- Metrics and guardrails to avoid gaming

## Code Example
```javascript
class SimpleCB {
  constructor() {
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
    this.failures = 0;
  }
  async call(fn, timeout = 5000) {
    if (this.state === 'OPEN') throw new Error('Circuit open');
    try {
      const t = new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, timeout, 'timeout'));
      const r = await Promise.race([fn(), t]);
      if (r === 'timeout') throw new Error('timeout');
      this.failures = 0;
      return r;
    } catch (e) {
      this.failures++;
      if (this.failures > 2) this.state = 'OPEN';
      throw e;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you communicate progress and risks to leadership?
- What experiments would you run to validate readiness and measure impact?
